<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kishangarh State</h1><p class="st-meta">1611 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Kishangarh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rathore Rajput state of Rajasthan celebrated for the Kishangarh school of painting and its iconic image of Bani Thani.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kishangarh was founded in 1611 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kishan-singh-of-kishangarh">Kishan Singh</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-marwar">Raja Udai Singh of Marwar</a>, who received the territory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> from the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>. The state took its name from him.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> served as Mughal mansabdars and later accepted British protection in 1818. The court became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> devotion and art: under Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawant-singh">Sawant Singh</a>, the poet Nagari Das, painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nihal-chand">Nihal Chand</a> created the elongated, lyrical Kishangarh style, epitomised by the portrait known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-thani">Bani Thani</a>. It was a fifteen-gun salute state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kishangarh joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a> in 1948. The town, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> district, is now known for its marble trade and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a> tradition that still bears its name.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kishangarh-state.jpg" alt="Kishangarh State" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kishangarh.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1611 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kishangarh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kishan Singh, Sawant Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dhundhari, Marwari</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kishangarh</dd></dl></aside></div>
